<spell-notebook "list usage">
	<header level="1">
		List
		<header>A list is used to group related content.</>
	</>
	<list-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#events">Events</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
				<ui-item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  items, links / buttons, ui-select, keyboard, accessibility, frameworks. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			A list's children are the one generic `<ui-item>`: the list tells each item how to render, so there is no
			`ui-list-item`.

			- An item with a `link` is a real link; in a `selection` list every item is a `<button>`; otherwise an item is a
			  plain block that can hold anything.
			- `icon` and `image` are shorthands for the item's icon and avatar; a `<ui-content>` holds a `<ui-header>` and a
			  `<ui-description>`.
			- A `<ui-list>` inside an item is its sub list, with the outer list's look (and numbering, when `ordered`).
			- `selected` (or Fomantic's `active`) marks the current item.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			`ui-select` fires when a link or button item of the list is activated (click, **Enter** or **Space**).

			- `detail.value` is the item's `value`, else its text; `detail.item` is the `<ui-item>`.
			- The list never moves `selected` itself, so the page decides what is chosen.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const list = document.querySelector("ui-list[selection]")
			list.addEventListener("ui-select", (event) => {
			  for (const item of list.querySelectorAll(":scope > ui-item")) {
			    item.toggleAttribute("selected", item === event.detail.item)
			  }
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			Every link or button item is its own tab stop; a list has no arrow-key navigation.

			- **Tab** moves to the next link or button item.
			- **Enter** follows a link item, or activates a button item (`ui-select`).
			- **Space** activates a button item (`ui-select`).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The shadow root holds a real `<ul>` (`<ol>` when `ordered`) with an explicit `role="list"`, since Safari drops
			  list semantics from unstyled lists. Each `<ui-item>` is a `listitem`.
			- Items with a `link` are real links; the `selected` one gets `aria-current="page"`. In a `selection` list the items
			  are `<button>`s and the selected one gets `aria-current="true"`.
			- `disabled` removes a link's `href` (and sets `aria-disabled`), or disables a button.
			- An item holding only an icon or image needs a name: put `aria-label` on the `<ui-item>`. The `image` shorthand
			  is decorative (`alt=""`).
			- If the element fails to render, it shows its items through a bare `<slot>`, so their text and links
			  stay; the `<ul>` / `<ol>` around them, the numbering, the list layout and `ui-select` go.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Define the elements first, then listen for `ui-select` with your framework's custom-event syntax.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-list selection onui-select={(event) => setChoice(event.detail.value)}>...</ui-list>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-list selection @ui-select="(event) => (choice = event.detail.value)">...</ui-list>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-list selection on-ui-chosen={(event) => setChoice(event.detail.value)}>
			  ...
			</ui-list>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
